//! The SOS call numbers: kernel-internal (spec.md §5.7).
//!
//! Every op number, rights bit, status tag and handle type in the system, in
//! one place. Exactly two things import this: the kernel's dispatch tables
//! (`kernel/core/`) and the `sos` wrapper module the kernel package exports to
//! userspace (`kernel/sysapi/`). Nothing else.
//!
//! Why it is shared rather than duplicated: the dispatch and the wrappers are
//! the two halves of one contract. Held apart, they skew, and a syscall-number
//! skew is not a compile error anywhere; it is a working call that does the
//! wrong thing. Held together, renumbering is a one-line edit that either
//! rebuilds both halves or fails.
//!
//! Why it is kernel-internal: the vDSO discipline. Numbers are not ABI.
//! Userspace links the exported `sos` module and calls methods; it never writes
//! `7` for an op, so the kernel may renumber freely between builds. The one
//! thing a process must agree with the kernel about is the calling convention
//! (§5.7: handle in a0, op in a7), and that lives in the per-arch HAL stub, not
//! here.
//!
//! Why these are enums and not statics: every family here is a closed named set
//! of values, which is what an Int-inheriting enum is for. It names the set,
//! pins the numbers so a reorder cannot renumber them, gives the dispatch an
//! exhaustive `match`, and turns the read at the trap boundary into
//! `from(raw:)`, a total function returning `E?`, so an unrecognized number is
//! data the kernel rejects rather than an index it trusts. Parallel statics
//! could be compared against anything and validated only by a hand-written `>
//! MAX` test.
//!
//! How a family is named. An op table or a rights set is named for the spec §2
//! object it belongs to, spelled out, plus the term-of-art suffix §5.7 uses:
//! `SystemOp`, `ProcessOp`, `ThreadOp`, `EventOp`, `WaiterOp`, `SystemRight`,
//! `ProcessRight`, and so on. No abbreviated object prefix (not `SysOp`, not
//! `ProcRight`, not `EvtRight`): the abbreviation saves four characters in a
//! file nobody types into and costs every reader the question of whether `Sys`
//! means the System object or the syscall. The rule extends to anything else
//! here named after an object (`ProcessStatusKind`, `process_status_word`,
//! `EventMode`): name the family after the row in the §2 table.
//!
//! How an op is named. Inside a family an op reads `object_operation`: the
//! object kind the op concerns leads, then the verb (`ProcessOp.ThreadCreate`,
//! `SystemOp.ClockGet`, `ClockOp.TimerCreate`), and the typed method and the C
//! export mirror it (`process.thread_create`, `sos_system_clock_get`). An op
//! whose receiver is the object it concerns keeps the bare verb, there being no
//! second noun to lead with: `Arm`, `Ack`, `Wait`, `Signal`, `Join`, `Start`,
//! `Exit`, `Yield`, `Now`, `Add`, `Remove`, `Shutdown`, `DebugPrint`,
//! `GetStatus`. Every op concerning a kind then sorts together, and a rights
//! bit named after an op (`ProcessRight.ThreadCreate`) reads exactly like the
//! op it gates. A right naming a capability rather than an op (`Transfer`,
//! `Mint`, `Read`, `Control`, `Attach`, `Debug`) keeps its own noun.
//!
//! Delivery is static linking through the manifest/module-path mechanism. A
//! genuinely mapped vDSO would change none of the above.
//!
//! # This file is the facade
//!
//! It holds no declaration: it re-exports what the leaves declare, so every
//! `import sosabi.{...}` in `kernel/core/`, `kernel/sysapi/` and `tests/` means
//! the same thing, and so does the `sosabi.` qualifier, which
//! `kernel/sysapi/src/system.saw` uses to reach `ENTRY_IMAGE` without binding
//! it bare.
//!
//! What keeps the dispatch and the wrappers from skewing is one package and one
//! importable name, not one file. Renumbering is a one-line edit that either
//! rebuilds both halves or fails.
//!
//! The modules, in dependency order. A file may import only those above it,
//! because an import cycle is a compile error naming the loop:
//!
//!   `types`    what a handle-table entry names, the per-kind handle aliases,
//!              `NO_HANDLE`, the handle word's encoding and `ENTRY_IMAGE`
//!   `rights`   the `*Right` enums with the asserts that pin the universal low
//!              byte, the two kind-blind predicates, and what every default set
//!              mints
//!   `ops`      the three universal ops and every per-object op table, plus the
//!              argument encodings that sit under the last op-table banner
//!              (`ClockType`, `MapAccess`, `EventMode`, `HandleInfo`,
//!              `HandleKind`, `HANDLE_KINDS`, `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`,
//!              `PIPE_MSG_HANDLES`, `PipeMsgMeta`)
//!   `records`  the wire records both halves copy: the wait record and its
//!              levels, the Clock/Timer records, the pipe records and the
//!              process-stats record
//!   `boot`     the boot-handle record and the boot handle set
//!   `status`   the status words, the fault reasons and the §8 process status
//!
//! Exactly one edge crosses a leaf boundary: `records` imports
//! `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`, `PIPE_MSG_HANDLES` and `PipeMsgMeta` from `ops`, because
//! `WaitBuffer` and `PipeMsgRecord` are sized in them. It is one-way (nothing
//! in `ops` names a record), so it is not a cycle, and `records.saw` carries
//! the note at the import.
//!
//! Every name is listed, rather than one `public import sosabi.<leaf>.*` per
//! leaf. A glob does not carry a `type` alias: it re-exports the statics, the
//! enums and the functions and silently omits the handle aliases, so
//! `MemoryHandle` would become a name-only type in
//! `kernel/sysapi/src/memory.saw` (no constructor, and no longer flowing to
//! `UInt`). The selective form carries an alias whole, in every position
//! (construction, annotation, field, parameter, return). The lists mirror the
//! leaves' public declarations exactly.
//!
//! So a declaration added to a leaf owes a name on the matching line here. That
//! is the bargain `kcore`'s facade and `sos`'s make too, and here it fails
//! loudly: an op number nothing republishes is unreachable at its first use,
//! not silently absent.
//!
//! The vDSO wall is unchanged: `kernel/sysapi/src/lib.saw` names the handful of
//! encodings a process is meant to write and re-exports those. The op numbers,
//! the rights bits and the handle aliases stay kernel-internal because nothing
//! republishes them.

public import sosabi.types.{ObjType, SystemHandle, ProcessHandle, ThreadHandle,
                            EventHandle, WaiterHandle, InterruptHandle,
                            ClockHandle, TimerHandle, MemoryHandle,
                            IoMemoryHandle, MappingHandle, PipeInletHandle,
                            PipeOutletHandle, PipeReplyHandle,
                            PipeRequestHandle, NO_HANDLE, HANDLE_WORD_BITS,
                            HANDLE_INDEX_BITS, HANDLE_GENERATION_BITS,
                            HANDLE_INDEX_MASK, HANDLE_GENERATION_MODULUS,
                            ENTRY_IMAGE}

public import sosabi.rights.{SystemRight, ProcessRight, ThreadRight,
                             EventRight, WaiterRight, InterruptRight,
                             ClockRight, TimerRight, MemoryRight,
                             IoMemoryRight, MappingRight, PipeInletRight,
                             PipeOutletRight, PipeReplyRight, PipeRequestRight,
                             rights_allow_transfer, rights_allow_mint,
                             root_system_rights, process_rights,
                             process_observe_rights, pipe_inlet_rights,
                             pipe_outlet_rights,
                             pipe_reply_rights, pipe_request_rights,
                             memory_rights, iomemory_rights, mapping_rights,
                             thread_rights, event_rights, waiter_rights,
                             interrupt_rights, clock_rights, timer_rights}

public import sosabi.ops.{RELEASE_OP, MINT_OP, INFO_OP, SystemOp,
                          ProcessOp, HandleInfo, HandleKind, HANDLE_KINDS,
                          BootTagForm,
                          ThreadOp, EventOp, WaiterOp, WAITER_ADD_ONESHOT,
                          WAITER_ADD_CONSUMING, WAITER_ADD_FLAGS, InterruptOp,
                          ClockOp, TimerOp, MemoryOp, IoMemoryOp, MappingOp,
                          PipeInletOp, PipeOutletOp, PipeReplyOp, PipeRequestOp,
                          ReplyWaitLeg, ClockType, MapAccess, MAP_ACCESS_MASK,
                          EventMode, SlabKind, ProtectionTier, PIPE_BODY_BYTES,
                          PIPE_MSG_HANDLES, PipeMsgMeta}

public import sosabi.records.{WaitTag, WAIT_HEADER_WORDS, WAIT_KEY_WORD,
                              WAIT_TAG_WORD, WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD,
                              WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS, WAIT_REQUEST_SLOT,
                              WAIT_MSG_SLOT_0, WaitBuffer, wait_header_bytes,
                              wait_meta_end_bytes, wait_prefix_bytes,
                              wait_record_bytes, OutletLevel, InletLevel,
                              ReplyLevel, CLOCK_NOW_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS,
                              CLOCK_NOW_TIME_SLOT, clock_now_record_bytes,
                              TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS,
                              TIMER_ARM_AFTER_SLOT, TIMER_ARM_INTERVAL_SLOT,
                              timer_arm_record_bytes, PIPE_CREATE_RECORD_WORDS,
                              PIPE_CREATE_INLET_WORD, PIPE_CREATE_OUTLET_WORD,
                              pipe_create_record_bytes, PipeMsgRecord,
                              PipeTakeRecord, pipe_msg_record_bytes,
                              pipe_msg_body_offset, pipe_take_record_bytes,
                              pipe_take_body_offset, PipeSendRecord,
                              pipe_send_record_bytes, PipeReplyWaitRecord,
                              pipe_reply_wait_record_bytes,
                              PROCESS_STATS_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS,
                              PROCESS_STATS_SYSCALLS_SLOT,
                              PROCESS_STATS_INTERRUPTS_SLOT,
                              PROCESS_STATS_FAULTS_SLOT,
                              PROCESS_STATS_PID_SLOT,
                              process_stats_record_bytes, StatsRow,
                              stats_row_bytes, StatsRegionHeader,
                              STATS_REGION_VERSION, stats_region_header_bytes,
                              STATS_REGION_MAP_RECORD_WORDS,
                              STATS_REGION_MAP_ROW_WORD,
                              stats_region_map_record_bytes}

public import sosabi.boot.{BOOT_HANDLE_RECORD_WORDS, BOOT_HANDLE_TAG_WORD,
                           BOOT_HANDLE_KIND_WORD, BOOT_HANDLE_HANDLE_WORD,
                           boot_handle_record_bytes, BootHandleKind,
                           ROOT_SYSTEM_HANDLE, NO_USER_STACK}

public import sosabi.status.{SosStatus, FaultReason, ProcessStatusKind,
                             process_status_word}
